CLOCK(1)                       P32W Unix                        CLOCK(1)

NAME
       clock - a clock with hands, in a window or on the screen

SYNOPSIS
       clock [seconds]

DESCRIPTION
       clock draws a clock's face and keeps its three hands at
       the time of day. Under the window manager (wm(1)) it has
       a window of its own, 200 by 220 dots; with no window
       manager it takes the whole of the video card's screen
       (video(4)), and the console's text is back when it ends.

       The face is as big as there is room for. On a face with
       room the hours are numbered and every minute has a mark;
       under it, where a line of text fits, the day and the time
       are written too.

       It goes until q is typed (with its window in front, under
       wm), or its window's box is clicked, or for the number of
       seconds given.

EXAMPLES
       clock
       win clock
              beside the terminals, not in one
       win -g 440x440 clock
              a big one
       clock 60
              for a minute

       Under wm it is also the Desk menu's Clock.

FILES
       /usr/src/cmd/clock.c       its source; /usr/examples/clock.c
                                  is the same thing at its shortest

SEE ALSO
       wm(1), win(1), date(1), gfx(3)

DIAGNOSTICS
       clock: /dev/video: and why, when it can have neither a
       window nor the screen: only root and group staff may draw
       on the screen, and one program at a time.
